  "worked_solution": "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficients matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|=-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $z$ is given by $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & k \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & k \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+2k)-3(1-k)+3(-2-0) \\\\  & =4k-3+3k-6 \\\\  & =7k-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{7k-9}{-15}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $z=2.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7k-9}{-15} & =2 \\\\ 7k-9 & =-30 \\\\ 7k & =-21 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
